# 9. Engineering Decision Log

This log records only genuine M1 decisions that the team has actually made, and justified deferments: decisions that cannot responsibly be made yet because the required evidence does not exist.

| ID | Type | Decision | Rationale | Trade-offs & Risks | Evidence & Later Consequence |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| DEC-001 | DECIDED | Define a controlled scope baseline at M1, explicit in-scope, out-of-scope, and deferred items. Context: The CivicConnect scope must be agreed and controlled before any architecture or implementation work begins. | A vague scope cannot be changed formally. A controlled baseline makes change visible and manageable. It also gives a clear starting point for requirements and the RTM. | Trade-offs: Some features stakeholders may want are deferred. This is acceptable because scope discipline is a project requirement, and a smaller delivered scope with evidence is stronger than a large undelivered one. Risks: Stakeholders may disagree with deferrals. Managed through the change request process. | Evidence: Scope baseline section in PED v1.0. Risk RISK-001 records scope creep as a managed risk. Later: Every change request must be assessed against this baseline. M4 final success evaluation compares delivered scope to this approved baseline. |
| DEC-002 | DECIDED | Use GitHub with branch protection on main, requiring two approvals from team members other than the author before any merge. Context: The team needs a shared code and artefact repository with access controls that support peer review and protect the controlled baseline. | GitHub is the platform explicitly referenced in the master brief. The team has existing familiarity. The two-reviewer approval model is a mandatory engineering control, not a preference. | Trade-offs: Every merge requires at least two other team members to review. This slows merges slightly but prevents unreviewed changes entering the controlled baseline. Risks: Rubber-stamp approvals (approving without reading) would defeat the control. Addressed by the meaningful review standard in the master brief. | Evidence: GitHub repository settings (protected main, required reviewers). Evidence to be shown live during presentation. Later: This model continues through M2, M3 and M4 for all substantive changes, including code, documentation, and engineering artefact updates. |
| DEC-003 | DEFERRED (Justified) | Deferred to M2. No technology stack is selected at M1. Context: The team must eventually select a technology stack (frontend framework, backend language, database, hosting). This decision is significant and affects the entire project. | Selecting a stack before requirements are complete forces the architecture to fit the tools rather than the problem. The master brief requires that technology decisions be justified against requirements and constraints — those are not yet fully established at M1. | Trade-offs: The team cannot begin implementation work until M2. This is acceptable because M1 is an engineering foundation milestone, not an implementation milestone. Risks: If deferred too long into M2, there may not be enough time to evaluate options properly. The team will treat this as the first priority in M2. | Evidence: Deferred decision recorded here in DEC-003. FEC-003 records the deployment environment as a forward consideration. Later: M2 must produce an Architecture Decision Record (ADR) for the stack selection with alternatives, trade-offs and evidence. |
| DEC-004 | DEFERRED (Justified) | Deferred to M2. No final architecture is selected at M1. Context: CivicConnect must have an architecture, how components are structured, how they communicate, and how data flows. This decision shapes the entire system. | Architecture without requirements is guesswork. The master brief explicitly states that M1 is not an architecture milestone. Committing to an architecture now would lock in decisions before the evidence needed to justify them exists. | Trade-offs: The team records architectural concerns in FEC-001 through FEC-007 so that relevant considerations are not forgotten when M2 begins. Risks: If FEC items are ignored in M2, the architecture may miss important constraints. The Forward Engineering register mitigates this. | Evidence: Deferred decision recorded here. FEC register captures the concerns that must inform the M2 architecture. Later: M2 must produce architecture diagrams and ADRs that trace back to requirements and NFRs defined in M1. |
| DEC-005 | DECIDED | Create and store a formal Team Working Agreement in the GitHub repository, reviewed and agreed by all three members. Context: The project must define a working process, how the team collaborates, resolves disagreements, distributes work, and meets deadlines. | An informal agreement cannot be evidenced. A documented agreement gives the team a reference point for resolving disputes and ensures assessors can see that the team operates with shared rules. | Trade-offs: Takes time to draft. This is offset by avoiding misaligned expectations later in the project. Risks: Agreement may not cover every situation. It should be treated as a living document and updated if gaps emerge. | Evidence: Team Working Agreement file committed to the repository with contributions from all three members. Later: Reviewed at each milestone gate. Any significant team process changes are recorded as updates to the agreement. |
| ADR-SEC-001 | DECIDED (M3) | Session login (POST /api/v1/auth/login) with BCrypt hashes and a 5-attempt lockout; 401 when not signed in; role rules per route; FilterStrategy per role. See docs/adr/ADR-SEC-001-session-login.md. | The M2 demo code used permitAll and trusted a browser-sent user id, so NFR003 and RISK-004 were not met. | CSRF off, so the cookie is SameSite=Strict (TD-004); lockout kept in memory (TD-001). | Evidence: SecurityConfig.java; TC-D-INT-01…04. Later: M4 may store the lockout in the database. |
| D2–D6 | DECIDED (M3, 12/10/2026) | Status transition permissions: Staff may take Open requests; only the assignee or Management may advance; illegal moves give 400 with no history row; null status is illegal; Management may make any legal move. See docs/desicions/D2-D6-status-transition-authority.md. | Completes FR011 and the open permission rules in ADR-DESIGN-001. | Stricter than "any Staff"; reassignment by Management writes no history row (DEF-E03, accepted). | Evidence: StatusTransitionGuard; TC-E-15 to TC-E-20; TC-D-05. |
| DEC-006 | DECIDED (M3, 18/10/2026) | Keep FR004/NFR001 in scope and deliver them by polling My Requests every 4 seconds instead of a server push (StatusVisibilityUpdater stays an unused stub). | Meets the 5-second criterion with the existing API and no extra infrastructure. | Extra GET requests every 4 s per open My Requests page; not real-time push. | Evidence: PR #126, statusChanges.test.js, timed demo 18/10. Deviation from ADR-DESIGN-001 recorded here. |
| DEC-007 | DECIDED (M3) | Use simulated staging (local Vite + Spring) for the release; Render setup kept for a later hosted deploy. | Allowed by the M3 brief; avoids free-tier sleep/expiry during assessment (RISK-006). | Does not prove behaviour on a public host. | Evidence: docs/staging/demo-prep.md, docs/staging/render-deploy.md. |
| DEC-008 | DECIDED (final, 18/10/2026) | Demo accounts only when civicconnect.demo-users.enabled is true (default only on the demo profile); password from DEMO_PASSWORD with no default outside demo. | Closes the RISK-004 release blocker: no known password on postgres/staging. | Local PostgreSQL demos must set DEMO_USERS_ENABLED and DEMO_PASSWORD. Residual: the tests cover the loader, not the settings of a hosted deploy (RISK-004). | Evidence: DemoUserLoader, application.yml, TC-D-08 to TC-D-10. |

**Final review (release v4.0.0, register owner: Donya):** DEC-003 and DEC-004 were deferred at M1 and resolved at M2 by ADR-TECH-001 and ADR-ARCH-001. All other decisions above are in force for the release. "No decision is still open" refers only to the decision records in this log: none is still DEFERRED or undecided. It does not mean that all work is closed. Open defects (DEF-E02), accepted technical debt (DEF-E03, TD-006, TD-007), verification gaps (RTM-010, RTM-015, RTM-017; manual-only timing for RTM-004 and RTM-014) and residual risks (Risk Register final review) are tracked in those records, not here.
